description:
Sean (2026-11-26): move the national scan from the 8 m contour-derived DEM (new-zealand-contour/dem_8m) to the LINZ 1 m LiDAR DEM (s3://nz-elevation, national mosaic new-zealand/new-zealand/dem_1m, surveys 2008-2026, lifecycle ongoing; 109 survey collections, all 1 m; nothing finer is published). Today only the three reference areas use it (resampled to 5 m). Reason: modelled reaches on the 8 m DEM drift off the real channel on the aerial photos.

Rule: before any national computation on the 1 m data, every dependent issue below is settled, so that all the per-reach information is extracted in ONE national pass. The blocking issues are linked as dependencies of this epic. Ask Sean before starting the national run.

Settle first (blocking):
- LiDAR coverage audit and fallback rule (new child);
- pipeline design and benchmark: resolution, tiling, memory, disk, run time (new child);
- the one-pass extraction spec: every per-reach attribute, plus what to keep so later features need no rerun (new child);
- rf-t26.11 tile edges (lakes split by tiles, lost REC2 inflows, Taupo gap) and rf-3ri.6 processing buffer: build into the new tiling;
- rf-33a.4 REC2 agreement gap (confluence snaps, plateau divides) and rf-3ri.7 LINZ river lines (use or burn them; test on the benchmark tile);
- rf-a33 DEM flats (check that 1 m LiDAR and the flat handling fix them, incl. water surfaces in the LiDAR DEM);
- rf-zmy.10 cross-sections (port to 1 m and extract in the same pass).

After the national pass (cheap re-runs, not blocking): MAF conflation + correction, scoring, models table, labels/sections placement (rf-r4a name matching), ML table, CV, a new card v5 (v1-v4 stay on the old rows), hydro attach (rf-6u2), external attach (rf-xyk.8 LINZ Topo50 waterfalls/roads, LDS key available), aerial coverage, rain weights/network/totals and the rain-inputs branch, web data/build, Pages.

Consider folding the three reference areas into the national run (one resolution everywhere), which removes the reference/national split in labels, web data and live rain.

Sean (2026-11-26): stop using our own modelled reaches and rely on the existing LINZ river segments. On the new Aerial base map the modelled reaches (D8 flow paths on the 8 m DEM, 5 m in the reference areas) align poorly with the river, while the LINZ river lines align much better.

LINZ source already in the pipeline: NZ River Name Lines (Pilot), LDS layer 103632 = NZ Topo50 river centrelines and canals with LINZ names, river_section_id and stream_order (linz.py; fetched per region by `web data --only linz` with the LDS key, raw features cached in data/nz/linz/<vid>.gpkg). Today it is only drawn as a map layer.

What hangs on the modelled reaches today (all keyed by reach row / viewer index):
- geometry and topology: down/up links, viewer arrays (S.c, d, u), run finder (core/runs.js), Top rivers;
- attributes: catchment area (routing), gradient and steepest 50 m (DEM along the D8 path), valley width / gorge depth / confinement (HAND), reach style (MB1997), MAF (REC1 flood statistics conflated onto reaches, rf-xyk.6 correction), geology, OSM waterfalls/roads (external attach);
- labels: known sections and guide research placed on reaches (labels.py, models.map_section, rf-aq6.27);
- ML: ml table, CV, every card (v1-v4 are tied to the current reach rows);
- rain: weights, network.npz, totals, the rain-inputs branch and the hourly job (viewer order);
- hydro flags (hydro.pkl), aerial coverage (<region>_aerial.json), cross-sections (rf-zmy.10).

Options to settle (try both on one region first, e.g. Kaimai or a West Coast tile, and measure against the aerial imagery: offset between line and channel, gradient and catchment-area differences, label coverage):
A. Replace: build the reach network from the LINZ lines (split at confluences into reaches of a few hundred metres; connectivity from the LINZ network, direction from the DEM), then sample the existing rasters along the LINZ lines: catchment area = flow accumulation snapped within a small buffer, elevation profile and gradient from the DEM (1 m LiDAR where it exists), HAND/valley metrics, REC2/MAF conflation onto the LINZ lines. Decide what to do where LINZ has no line (small unnamed headwaters: keep modelled lines below some area, or drop them).
B. Middle path: burn the LINZ lines into the DEM before routing (stream burning / AGREE), so the modelled network follows the LINZ lines while keeping one consistent topology and catchment areas; everything downstream of routing stays as it is. Needs a national re-route (~4 h) but no change to the reach model.

Watch for: braided rivers (several LINZ channels: pick the main one), lakes and canals in the LINZ network, gaps and direction errors in the 1:50k lines, reaches crossing tile edges (rf-t26.11), the REC2 agreement gap (rf-33a.4).

Consequences: a national rebuild (scoring, models table, ML table and CV, a new card v5 trained on the new reaches; v1-v4 stay with the old rows), rain-inputs refresh, hydro/aerial/xsec re-attach, web data and build, Pages. Ask Sean before starting the national rebuild.
